### 1. `app/helpers/time_helper.rb`

```ruby
module TimeHelper
  # Renders a <time> element carrying the raw timestamp in its +datetime+
  # attribute so the local-time Stimulus controller can put it into words in
  # the browser, in the viewer's own timezone. The server-rendered HTML stays
  # identical for everyone, so fragments embedding times remain shareable.
  #
  # Styles:
  #   :datetime     "1:42 PM", "yesterday", "5 days ago", or "Sep 12"
  #   :ago          "5 minutes ago", "yesterday", "5 days ago"
  #   :until        "today", "tomorrow", "in 3 days"
  #   :date         "Sep 12" or "Sep 12, 2024"
  #   :long_date    "Sep 12, 2025"
  #   :weekday_date "Friday, September 12"
  #   :time         "1:42 PM"
  #   :short        "9/12/25, 1:42 PM"
  def local_time_tag(time, style, **attributes)
    attributes[:data] = { controller: "local-time", local_time_style_value: style }.merge(attributes.fetch(:data, {}))

    tag.time(fallback_content(time), datetime: utc_iso8601(time), **attributes)
  end

  private
    def utc_iso8601(time)
      time.respond_to?(:utc) ? time.utc.iso8601 : time.iso8601
    end

    def fallback_content(time)
      if time.respond_to?(:utc)
        time.utc.strftime("%b %-d, %Y, %-l:%M %p UTC")
      else
        time.strftime("%b %-d, %Y")
      end
    end
end
```

Wait — `tag.time(content, options)` — Rails TagHelper: `tag.time("text", datetime: ...)`. Options after content. My signature: `tag.time(fallback_content(time), datetime: ..., **attributes)` — attributes includes data and class. But careful: I merged data into attributes and also pass `datetime:` explicitly — order fine.

